On mobile, the creator should be able to start and manage a live broadcast without needing a desktop. However, some professional controls such as complex scene production will be better supported through OBS and desktop software.

The system must also handle poor connectivity: show clear connection status, recover playback, and prevent a temporary network interruption from incorrectly marking the entire event as successfully completed.
12. Live streaming states and lifecycle
Every broadcast needs a reliable lifecycle so that the creator, viewers and administrators see the same actual status.
State	Meaning
draft	Broadcast is being configured
scheduled	Broadcast has a scheduled start time
preparing	Creator is connecting and testing
live	Broadcast is actively streaming
interrupted	Temporary connection interruption
ending	Broadcast is being closed
processing	Recording and replay are being processed
pending_review	Content requires moderation review
ended	Broadcast has finished
ready	Replay is ready for permitted viewing
failed	Broadcast or processing failed
cancelled	Scheduled broadcast was cancelled
suspended	Platform has suspended the session


The system should use real ingest and processing events to change states. It must not use a frontend timer to pretend a broadcast is live.
